{
  "methodology": "[DATA] cross-sectional norms computed from firm datasets. leg_norms_zscored: bs_asset_growth/bs_issuance are ALREADY SIGN-INVERTED, global cross-sectional z-scores (mean~0,std~1 universe-wide) from rnd/panel/capstone_legs.parquet (min_legs=5 canonical 7-leg construction), latest available obs per symbol. Convention (confirmed via KPIGREEN cross-check against raw fundamentals below): these two legs are built quality-style so HIGHER RAW asset-growth/dilution -> MORE NEGATIVE leg value (consistent with the academic asset-growth/issuance anomalies where low-growth, low-issuance predicts higher forward returns). A sector median far from 0 means that sector is SYSTEMATICALLY high/low-growth or high/low-dilution vs the whole market on that dimension -- read a negative sector median as 'high raw asset growth/dilution is NORMAL here', not as a flag. quality_cfo_pat and value_EY are raw ratios (CFO/PAT multiple, earnings yield E/P) -- NOT sign-inverted. bs_issuance_flagged_incidence_pct = % of sector names with bs_issuance<-1 (heavy-issuance tail) since the leg is sparse/event-driven and medians alone don't show tail incidence. implied_pe_from_median_EY is a readability aid (1/median EY, sane range only). fund_norms_raw_pit: computed directly from data/fundamentals/MASTER_fundamentals_pit.parquet, latest fiscal year vs prior fiscal year per symbol, winsorized at sector 2nd/98th pct to blunt single-name outliers -- these are RAW (not sign-inverted) so read directly (e.g. Power fixed_asset_growth_yoy median=+1.4% is the SECTOR-WIDE incumbent-utility norm; a scaling small-cap EPC/IPP name within Power will run far above this -- macro-sector norms are necessarily blunt for a fast-growing sub-segment, see sub_sector caveat). Sectors with n<5 symbols after merge/dropna are excluded (insufficient sample -- do not use as norm). KNOWN GAP: sub_sector-level norms are NOT computed here -- most sub_sectors have n<5 in this universe (e.g. Power Infrastructure = 3 names: ANZEN, KPIGREEN, ORIANA) so macro_sector is the finest reliable statistical cut; the analyst-agent must still apply business-model judgment (KB layer) on top for a sub-segment that behaves differently from its macro-sector's median member. sector_map source: data/universe/sector_map.parquet (2825 symbols, macro_sector/sub_sector taxonomy).",
